virglrenderer: big texture uploads through a pixel unpack buffer

What: vrend_renderer_transfer_write_iov gathers the guest's pages for a plain 2D
or rectangle texture upload of 64 KiB or more straight into a pixel unpack
buffer (orphaned each time, mapped for writing) and uploads from it, instead of
into a malloc'd copy that glTexSubImage2D reads from client memory. Only where
the old path needed no CPU conversion: core profile, not GLES, not compressed,
not flipped (y_0_top), not Z24X8, one layer. The guest's row stride is packed
as in the temporary copy. A failed map or unmap takes the old path; the unpack
binding is put back. OMACVM_VIRGL_UPLOAD_PBO=0 turns it off.
Why: from client memory Apple's GL copies the data once more and lays it out
for the GPU on the CPU. Omarchy's frozen screen for screenshots (hyprpicker)
uploads a whole display per frame: 21.6 MB at 2940x1840, 47-51 % of QEMU's main
loop on a MacBook Air M2 in transfer_write_iov (memmove 28 %, the gather 16 %,
glTexSubImage2D). Through the buffer the upload takes about half the main
loop's time (M4 Max: 1.13 -> 0.55 ms for 2940x1840 in 331 pieces); on the Air
the frozen screen with a moving pointer went from 36-39 % to 31 % of the main
loop, its late timers from 556 to 202. Every app drawing on the CPU (shm
buffers) gains.
Upstream: could go upstream (Apple-specific gain); not sent.
Tests: Tests/virgl/test-upload-pbo.c: whole textures and boxes, strides wider
than the box, 1 and 4 bytes a pixel, odd widths, guest memory in one piece and
in many of odd sizes, an offset, a small upload, a rectangle texture, the same
texture again: read back and compared with the guest's bytes, with the buffer
and with OMACVM_VIRGL_UPLOAD_PBO=0. index-range-writes.py: the buffer is
vrend's own.
--- a/src/vrend/vrend_renderer.c
+++ b/src/vrend/vrend_renderer.c
@@ -12132,7 +12132,47 @@
    }
    return false;
 }
+
+/* OmacVM: big texture uploads go through a pixel unpack buffer. The guest's
+ * pages are gathered straight into it (one CPU copy), and the GPU puts the
+ * pixels into the texture's layout. From client memory, Apple's GL copies the
+ * data once more and lays it out on the CPU: a 2940x1840 window (21.6 MB, what
+ * a frozen screen in screenshot mode uploads on every pointer move) took about
+ * twice the main thread's time. Plain 2D uploads only (no flip, no CPU
+ * conversion); OMACVM_VIRGL_UPLOAD_PBO=0 turns it off. */
+#define VREND_UPLOAD_PBO_MIN (64 * 1024)
+static GLuint vrend_upload_pbo;
 
+static bool vrend_upload_pbo_wanted(void)
+{
+   static int wanted = -1;
+   if (wanted < 0) {
+      const char *env = getenv("OMACVM_VIRGL_UPLOAD_PBO");
+      wanted = !(env && !strcmp(env, "0"));
+   }
+   return wanted;
+}
+
+/* A write-only mapping of SIZE bytes of the upload buffer, bound to
+ * GL_PIXEL_UNPACK_BUFFER (NULL: none; nothing is left bound then). */
+static void *vrend_upload_pbo_map(uint64_t size)
+{
+   void *map;
+
+   if (!vrend_upload_pbo)
+      glGenBuffers(1, &vrend_upload_pbo);
+   if (!vrend_upload_pbo)
+      return NULL;
+   glBindBuffer(GL_PIXEL_UNPACK_BUFFER, vrend_upload_pbo);
+   /* new storage each time: the GPU may still read the last upload */
+   glBufferData(GL_PIXEL_UNPACK_BUFFER, (GLsizeiptr)size, NULL, GL_STREAM_DRAW);
+   map = glMapBufferRange(GL_PIXEL_UNPACK_BUFFER, 0, (GLsizeiptr)size,
+                          GL_MAP_WRITE_BIT | GL_MAP_INVALIDATE_BUFFER_BIT);
+   if (!map)
+      glBindBuffer(GL_PIXEL_UNPACK_BUFFER, 0);
+   return map;
+}
+
 static int vrend_renderer_transfer_write_iov(struct vrend_context *ctx,
                                              struct vrend_resource *res,
                                              const struct iovec *iov, int num_iovs,
@@ -12228,7 +12268,36 @@
